    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d767">

                <pb facs="0812" n="802" sameAs="frus:frus1949v09/pg802"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.516/0–2349: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister-Counselor of
                        Embassy in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Clark</hi>), <hi
                        rendition="#italic">at Canton</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-07-22T20:00:00-04:00">July 22, 1949—8 p.
                            m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Telcan 487. Re last para Cantel 637, June 23, no record Chi Emb opening here
                    question settlement reverse-lend lease. Subject mentioned in general terms only
                    during course war accounts negotiations summer 1948.<anchor n="45"
                        xml:id="d767-f1"/> Dept unaware basis rumored Chi Govt claims since no
                    reverse lend-lease transactions reported by Chinese. Urinfo doubtful they wld be
                    given favorable consideration if presented. Any event Dept eld not consider
                    settlement reverse lend-lease separately from overall settlement war
                    accounts.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Acheson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d767-f1" n="45" xml:id="d767-f1-content">
                <p>For correspondence on this subject, see <ref target="frus1948v08#pg_686">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1948, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">viii</hi>, pp. 686</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
